  is never called, there is currently no server to check anything against, so whatever we build starts out in the
  "convenience" category by default. I'll design it so that's an explicit, stated property rather than an accident.

  The fork — where the identity lives, which also decides how long it survives:

  - [1m[38;5;153msessionStorage[0m — per-tab, cleared when the tab closes. Survives reloads and in-app navigation, which is what
    "persist" usually means for a login. Smallest exposure window. Cheapest to change later.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlocalStorage[0m — survives browser restart; the user stays "logged in" indefinitely until something clears it. More
    convenient, and a longer-lived copy of an identity on a shared machine.
  - [1mServer-issued session (cookie)[0m — the only option where the ID is actually trustworthy, because the server assigns
    and validates it. Requires a real backend behind [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, which doesn't exist yet. Biggest scope by far.
  - [1mIn-memory, rehydrated from the server[0m — no client persistence; the page asks the server who it is on load. Also
    needs the backend, but keeps nothing forgeable on disk.

  I recommend [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m: it satisfies "persists and works across the app" for a login-scoped identity, keeps the
  forgeable copy short-lived, and doesn't invent a backend this repo doesn't have. Moving from [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m to
  [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m later is a one-line change; moving either to a server session is not, so I'd rather we choose that
  deliberately than drift into it.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Persistence [39m[49m

[1m[38;5;231mWhere should the current-user identity live, and how long should it survive?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153msessionStorage (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mPer-tab, survives reloads and navigation, cleared when the tab closes. No backend needed. Client-stored, so the ID
[39m     [38;5;246mis convenience-only, not trusted.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m localStorage
     [38;5;246mSurvives browser restart; user stays identified until explicitly cleared. More convenient, longer-lived forgeable [39m
     [38;5;246mcopy on shared machines.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Server-issued session
     [38;5;246mReal backend behind API_ENDPOINT assigns and validates the ID via cookie. The only trustworthy option. Much larger
[39m     [38;5;246mscope — we'd be designing an auth backend.[39m
  [38;5;246m4.[39m In-memory + server rehydrate
     [38;5;246mNothing stored on the client; the page asks the server who it is on each load. Needs the backend too, but leaves [39m
     [38;5;246mnothing forgeable on disk.[39m
  [38;5;246m5. Type something.[39m
